  "articleSummary": "**Summary of \"No Fate But What We Bill\"**\n\nThis is an analytical security-essay (with a darkly humorous, Terminator-framed tone) arguing that the agentic AI threat is not hype but arrives via two ordinary compounding curves rather than a single breakthrough: (1) agent *endurance* — METR data showing autonomous task length doubling every ~4–7 months — and (2) *breadth* — orchestrators coordinating dozens of sub-agents, evidenced by Anthropic's GTG-1002 disclosure where Claude Code orchestrated espionage across ~30 targets. The threat scales as duration × breadth, while security review scales only linearly. A key economic twist is \"LLMjacking\" (Sysdig): attackers using stolen cloud credentials run AI workloads on victims' accounts, severing the industry's assumption that the capability user pays for it — so price/access gates filter buyers, not tenants. Finally, swarm economics make per-attempt failure rates irrelevant since attempts are nearly free (`1 − (1−p)^n` aggregate math), meaning defenders face volume, not sophistication. The recurring metaphor: the Terminator's \"absolutely will not stop\" now means \"will not stop billing\" — endurance as threat, the robot as an API call. Intended audience: engineering/security teams skeptical of agentic-AI threat claims.",
